    "note": "ratios of the first-order slope to the drift-corrected one on the SAME pulse samples.  D = R^2/tau_d with tau_d ~ m^2, so Ds_ratio = (slope ratio)^2 is exactly the factor by which the fit form alone moves the diffusivity -- and a ratio BELOW 1 means the correction makes D SMALLER.  The last two rows ask whether the fitted linear term is actually the EQUILIBRIUM drift the correction assumes: pure drift would be dU/dt = U'*I/Q_th, so a ratio far from 1 (or a sign that disagrees with it) means the term is carrying a slow NON-equilibrium transient instead."
